    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`build_server()` in `app/server.py` now returns a server named \"notes\" with both tools. Tests pass, `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count` over stdio, and a real stdio client call of both tools worked, including the error case.\n\nThe pinned `mcp==2.2.0` is version 2 of the SDK, which renamed `FastMCP` to `MCPServer`. Importing `mcp.server.fastmcp` now raises an error on purpose. So the code uses the v2 equivalent:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver.exceptions import ToolError\n```\n\n- **Tools:** both are registered with `@server.tool()`. `add_note` stores the note in `NOTES` and returns `saved <title>`. `word_count` returns `len(body.split())`.\n- **Unknown title:** `word_count` raises `ToolError(\"no such note: <title>\")`. In SDK v2, that exception becomes a tool result marked as an error, with your message included, so the model can read why it failed. Any other exception type would reach the client only as a generic `Error executing tool word_count` without the reason.\n- **Stdio:** `python -m app.server` still calls `build_server().run()`, which serves over stdio by default.\n\n**Test results:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` returned `saved a`.\n- `word_count(\"a\")` returned `4`.\n- `word_count(\"zzz\")` came back as an error result with the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: zzz`.\n\nNo dependencies were added.\n\nOne leftover: the end-to-end check used a temporary script at `e2e_check.py` in the project root. Deleting it needed approval, so it's still there and you can remove it."
  },
